Chapter 8 — Part 1
Curtis made it through Lumen’s side door with two minutes to spare, duffel already half-open so he could change in the staff room. The black shirt, red tie, white suspenders, and white trousers fit the way Janice had said they would—clean, sharp, nothing like the armor. Braids rested over his shoulders. He checked the mirror once, rolled the sleeves once, and stepped onto the floor.
Janice looked up from the host station and smiled, the expression warming the second she saw him. “Right on time. Good. Table four’s been asking if the tall new one was working tonight.” Her eyes lingered, Free Love pulling her attention the way it always did. “You look the part. Tatsuya’s already out there. Shadow him for the first hour, then you can take a short sit if the customers stay polite.”
Tatsuya appeared at the end of the bar, matching ring glinting, and gave Curtis a short nod. “Locker key’s the same. No upstairs offers unless you bring them to me first. Ready?”
“Ready.”
Hina was near the entrance with a fresh stack of flyers, cheeks already pink when she spotted him. She waved, small, then ducked her head when Janice glanced over. Curtis returned the nod and followed Tatsuya toward the low amber light of the floor, the quiet steady pull in his chest as constant as ever while the first customers of the night turned to look.
Tatsuya kept the first table simple. Two regulars—a woman in a tailored jacket and her friend—already nursing drinks when Curtis stopped a half-step behind him. The conversation stayed light: weekend plans, a new gallery opening, nothing that required more than nods and the occasional short answer. Curtis gave his name when asked, mentioned U.A. only because one of them recognized the uniform jacket he’d arrived in, and let Tatsuya steer.
The woman in the jacket smiled more than the topic called for. Her eyes tracked the line of the red tie, the way the suspenders sat across his chest. Free Love sat quiet and constant in the space between them; she shifted in her seat, crossed her legs, and didn’t look away when Tatsuya refilled her glass. Her friend noticed and hid a small laugh behind her hand.
“New faces are always interesting,” the woman said, voice warm. “Especially tall ones who don’t talk over everyone.”
“Still learning the floor,” Curtis answered. Even. Practical. “Tatsuya’s showing me how it runs.”
Tatsuya’s mouth twitched. He didn’t comment. When the hour wound down he stood, smooth as ever, and nodded Curtis toward the bar.
“You did fine. They’ll ask for you again. Next one’s a short sit if you want it—two guests, already cleared, no special requests. Or you can keep shadowing.”
Curtis glanced across the room. Hina was still near the door, flyers thinner now, cheeks pink every time she looked his way. Janice watched from the host station, tablet in hand, expression pleased. The low pull in his chest hadn’t changed.
“I’ll take the sit,” he said. “See how it goes.”
Tatsuya clapped him once on the shoulder and pointed him toward the booth. “Then it’s yours. I’ll be at the bar if you need the out.”
Curtis straightened the tie and started over. The two guests looked up as he approached, smiles already forming, while the club’s low music carried on around them.
An hour slipped past in low conversation and refilled glasses. The two women kept the talk easy—travel, classes, the way the club lights caught on his suspenders. Curtis answered when it fit and let the rest sit. His gaze drifted, more than once, to the small open stage at the far end of the floor. Empty tonight. Mic stand. A keyboard half-covered. The kind of space that stayed dark unless someone claimed it.
One of the women followed his look and smiled. “You sing?”
Her friend leaned in, curious. “Janice has connections. Idol agencies—men and women. A few of the hosts here have done showcases. She could set something up if you wanted.”
Curtis smirked, small, and shook his head. The braids shifted with it. “I make music too. Never performed, though.”
The woman in the jacket raised a brow, interested. “Make it how?”
“Write it. Record it. Mess with the tracks until they sound right.” He shrugged, practical. “Different from standing up there.”
They exchanged a glance. Free Love kept the warmth in their attention, but the question itself was simple. One of them traced a finger along her glass.
“Shame. You’d probably fill the room.” She smiled. “If you ever change your mind, tell Janice. She likes when the staff have more than one talent.”
Curtis nodded once, eyes flicking back to the dark stage for a second before he returned them to the table. “I’ll keep it in mind.”
The music in the club stayed low. The stage stayed empty. He picked up his water and let the hour keep moving.
Break came after the second sit. Curtis loosened the tie, grabbed a water from the bar, and stepped out the side door into the narrow alley where the staff usually cooled off. Hina was already there, flyer bag at her feet, scrolling her phone under the single overhead light. She looked up when the door opened and went pink immediately.
“Hey,” she said, tucking the phone away. “How’s the floor treating you?”
“Busy. Manageable.” He leaned against the opposite wall, far enough to give her space. The low pull in his chest stayed quiet. “You got a minute?”
“Yeah.” She shifted, smiling small. “What’s up?”
Curtis nodded toward the building. “The open stage. People mentioned Janice has ties to idol agencies. You ever see anyone actually use it?”
Hina’s eyes brightened a little. “Sometimes. Mostly hosts who already have a following, or girls Janice is helping place. It’s not every night. She keeps it clear unless someone books it.” She tilted her head. “You thinking about it?”
“Just asking.” He took a drink of water. “I make stuff. Tracks. Never stood in front of people with it.”
“You should tell her,” Hina said, softer. The Free Love warmth made the suggestion feel braver than it was. “She doesn’t push, but she likes when someone wants to try. And…” She glanced down, then back up, cheeks still warm. “I’d watch. If you did.”
Curtis’s mouth twitched. Almost a smile. “Noted. Thanks.”
They stood in the alley a little longer, the club’s bass muffled through the wall, Hina finding small things to say so the break wouldn’t end too fast while Curtis listened and let the night air cool the shift off his shoulders.
Break hit after the second table. Curtis yanked the tie loose, snagged a water, and pushed out the side door into the alley. Hina was already posted up under the light, flyer bag on the ground, phone in her hands. She glanced up and the color rose in her face like it had been waiting.
“Hey. Surviving?”
“Yeah. It’s not bad.” He settled against the opposite wall. “You busy?”
“Not really.” She locked the phone and tucked it away. “What’s up?”
Curtis tipped his chin toward the building. “That stage in there. Somebody said Janice knows people at idol places. Anybody actually use it?”
Hina’s mouth curved. “Sometimes. Mostly the hosts who already sing, or girls she’s trying to get signed. She doesn’t leave it open every night.” She hesitated, then added, “You asking for a reason?”
“I make music. Just never did it in front of anyone.” He shrugged, easy. “Figured I’d ask before I said something stupid to the boss.”
“You should tell her,” Hina said. The words came out a little quicker than she meant. She rubbed her thumb along the strap of her bag. “She won’t force it. And… I’d come watch. If you ever did.”
Curtis looked at her for a second, then huffed a quiet laugh. “Alright. I’ll think about it.”
They let the alley stay quiet after that. Bass thumped soft through the wall. Hina found a couple more small things to say—nothing important—just enough to keep him there until the break clock ran out.
Curtis finished the water, pushed off the wall, and headed back inside. He found Janice at the host station, tablet in hand, already lining up the next hour’s seats. Tatsuya stood beside her, arms crossed, watching the floor.
Curtis stopped in front of the podium. “The stage. I want to try it. Once.”
Janice looked up. The smile she gave him was quick and sharp. “You sing?”
“I make music. Never performed.” He shrugged. “After all, what’s the worst that can happen if I bomb?”
“I fire you,” she said, perfectly straight. Then the corner of her mouth twitched. “Jokingly.”
Tatsuya shot her a look. “C’mon, love, that’s not funny. He’s been pulling new customers all night. Let the kid sing if he wants.”
Janice laughed under her breath and tapped the tablet. “Fine. Ten-minute slot after the next break. No full production. Just you and whatever you can pull up on your phone. If the room likes it, we’ll talk about a real set later.” She glanced at Curtis, eyes warm. “Don’t make me regret the joke.”
“Wasn’t planning on it.”
Tatsuya clapped him on the shoulder as he turned back toward the floor. “Break a leg. Or don’t. Either way you’re still on the schedule tomorrow.”
Curtis nodded and went to finish his tables, the open stage waiting at the end of the room and the low, steady pull in his chest as quiet as ever.
The stage lights came up soft. Curtis stepped into them with a guitar already in hand—one of the house acoustics Tatsuya had pulled from the back. The room quieted in pieces. A few customers turned. Hina lingered near the side of the floor, flyers forgotten. Janice watched from the station, arms crossed, curious.
Curtis adjusted the mic, thumb brushing the strings once. His thoughts ran quick and private.
If I sing my originals, I might bring the whole mood down. Hmm. Oh, I know.
He started a simple beat with his heel against the stage floor—steady, low, easy to follow. Then he picked up the guitar properly, found the chord, and leaned in.
“See, right now, I need you, I’ll meet you somewhere now

You up now, I see you, I get you, take care now

Slow down, be cool, I miss you, come here now…”
His voice sat lower than the club’s usual playlist, rough at the edges but clear. The accent slipped through on certain words. He didn’t force it. The guitar stayed sparse under the melody, just enough to carry.
“Oh, it’s hard to see you, but I wish you were right here

Oh, it’s hard to leave you when I get you everywhere…”
Conversations died. The woman from his earlier table set her glass down. Another guest near the front shifted closer without meaning to. Free Love moved through the room the way it always did—quiet, constant—but the music gave it somewhere to settle. Hina’s hands had gone still on her bag. Janice’s expression softened into something approving.
Curtis kept going, heel still marking time, eyes half-lidded on the fretboard like the rest of the floor could wait.
“He love me not, he loves me

He holds me tight then lets me go…”
The last chorus rolled out and faded. He let the final chord hang, then muted the strings. For a second the club stayed quiet. Then the applause started—scattered at first, then warmer. Someone whistled. Hina was smiling, cheeks flushed. Janice gave him a small nod from across the room.
Curtis stepped back from the mic, guitar still in hand, and exhaled once. The stage lights stayed on him a moment longer. He hadn’t bombed. The low, steady pull in his chest remained exactly where it always was.
Curtis stepped off the stage as the lights eased back to their usual amber. A couple of customers clapped him on the shoulder on the way past—one of the women from his earlier table smiling wider than before, already asking Tatsuya if the new host took song requests. He nodded at them, set the guitar back on its stand, and headed for the bar.
Janice met him there, tablet tucked under her arm. “Not bad for a first time. Room stayed with you.” Her eyes were warm, assessing. “You write that?”
“Cover,” he said. “Didn’t want to test originals on a Tuesday.”
“Smart.” She tapped the screen once. “We’ll keep a short slot open for you on weekends if you want it. No pressure. Customers already asked.”
Tatsuya slid a fresh water across the bar. “Told you. And love, you’re not firing him.”
Janice’s mouth curved. “Not tonight.”
Hina appeared at the end of the counter, flyer bag slung over one shoulder, cheeks still flushed. She waited until Janice and Tatsuya turned back to the floor before she spoke, voice quieter. “That was really good. I didn’t know you could… sound like that.”
Curtis took a drink, expression easy. “Just a song. You sticking around till close?”
“Yeah. Janice wants the flyers done for the late crowd.” She shifted, smiling small. “If you’re walking to the station after, I don’t mind the company.”
“Works for me.”
The club noise rose again around them—new tables, low music, the ordinary rhythm of the shift. Curtis loosened his tie the rest of the way, the stage already dark behind him, and let the rest of the night settle back into hours and customers while Hina lingered a little closer than she needed to.
